---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CHANNEL_LIST, EDITIONS } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="About this desk"
  description="How OG Signal is produced: researched from primary sources, drafted by a model from a fixed fact pack, then checked number by number before anything is published."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="page-head">
        <p class="label">About</p>
        <h1>A desk that shows its work.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          OG Signal covers artificial intelligence, language models, machine learning and the
          technology underneath them. It files three editions a day and keeps every claim attached to
          the source it came from.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why it exists</h2>
        <p>
          Most AI coverage is either a press release with a headline or a thread that cannot be
          checked. The useful middle is narrow: what was announced, what the numbers actually say,
          what the vendor did not disclose, and what to watch next. That is the whole brief here.
        </p>

        <h2>How an edition is made</h2>
        <p>
          Each edition runs in four steps, in this order, and a run that fails a step publishes
          nothing.
        </p>
        <dl>
          <dt>One. Research</dt>
          <dd>
            Candidate stories are found across the four channels, then each one is opened at its
            primary source: the company's own announcement, a filing, an official research post or a
            pressroom release. A headline alone is not enough to proceed.
          </dd>
          <dt>Two. A fact pack</dt>
          <dd>
            Everything allowed into the copy is written down first, as a list of sourced statements
            with the URL beside each one. If a number is not in the fact pack it cannot appear in the
            article. Arithmetic is done and recorded at this stage.
          </dd>
          <dt>Three. Writing</dt>
          <dd>
            The articles are drafted by a language model from that fact pack alone. It is given no
            other context and no ability to browse, which is what keeps it inside the facts.
          </dd>
          <dt>Four. Verification</dt>
          <dd>
            Every draft is read against the fact pack line by line, and a script checks that each
            number in the copy exists in the pack, that every cited source matches, that the word
            count and structure hold, and that the banned-phrase list is clean. Anything that fails
            is rejected rather than softened.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <h2>What that means for you</h2>
        <p>
          The prose is machine-drafted and human-checked. The facts are sourced and dated. Each story
          shows how many claims were checked and when, and a story resting on a single primary
          announcement says so on the page instead of implying wider corroboration.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a digest. It summarises and attributes; it does not republish anyone's reporting,
          and it does not use other people's photographs. Where a story is thin, the gap is described
          rather than filled.
        </p>

        <h2>Cadence</h2>
        <p>
          Three editions a day, filed on IST: the <strong>{EDITIONS.morning.label}</strong> at
          {' '}{EDITIONS.morning.cut}, the <strong>{EDITIONS.afternoon.label}</strong> at
          {' '}{EDITIONS.afternoon.cut} and the <strong>{EDITIONS.night.label}</strong> at
          {' '}{EDITIONS.night.cut}. A quiet window produces a shorter edition, not a padded one.
        </p>

        <h2>Channels</h2>
        <p>
          {CHANNEL_LIST.map((c) => c.label).join(', ')}. A story is filed under the channel where the
          substance sits, not where the announcement landed.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Errors are corrected on the page and logged in public. The standards used are written out in
          the <a href="/editorial">editorial standards</a>, and the
          {' '}<a href="/corrections">corrections log</a> is always visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose dl {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }
  .prose dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink-mute);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .prose dd {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
